# PowerShell Silent Failures

The defects below were reproduced on **Windows PowerShell 5.1**. None raises
an error at the point of the mistake: the script keeps running, exits `0`, and
the data is simply gone — the symptom surfaces somewhere else, in a different
program.

## 1. `-match` against an array does not populate `$Matches`

`string -match pattern` is a boolean test that sets `$Matches`.
`array -match pattern` is a **filter**: it returns the elements that match. A
non-empty result is truthy, so an `if` guard passes — but `$Matches` was never
touched.

### Reproduction

```console
PS> $arr = @("package: name='com.example.demo' versionCode='7' versionName='1.2'",
             "sdkVersion:'26'")

PS> if ($arr -match "versionName='([^']+)'") { $Matches[1] }
Cannot index into a null array.        <-- guard passed, $Matches never set

PS> ($arr -join "`n") -match "versionName='([^']+)'"; $Matches[1]
True
1.2
```

### Why it bites

The output of a native command is an **array of lines**, so the
idiomatic-looking form is wrong exactly where it is most tempting:

```powershell
$output = & $aapt2 dump badging $apk      # array, one element per line
if ($output -match "versionName='([^']+)'") { $Matches[1] }   # always empty
```

In the real case this made a build script record package, `versionName` and
`versionCode` as `"unknown"` for **every** build. Under
`$ErrorActionPreference = "Stop"` the null index does worse than return
nothing — it aborts the script immediately *after* a successful compile, which
points suspicion at the build step rather than the parse.

### The fix

Join to a single string before matching:

```powershell
$text = (& $tool @args) -join "`n"
if ($text -match "versionName='([^']+)'") { $verName = $Matches[1] }
```

Rule of thumb: **`$Matches` is only trustworthy when the left operand is a
single string.** If you cannot see that it is one, join it first.

## 2. `Set-Content -Encoding UTF8` writes a BOM

Windows PowerShell 5.1's `-Encoding UTF8` means *UTF-8 with BOM*; that cmdlet
has no BOM-less UTF-8 option.

### Reproduction

First three bytes of the written file:

```console
$json | Set-Content $p -Encoding UTF8
  -> 239,187,191                  <-- EF BB BF, the BOM

[IO.File]::WriteAllText($p, $json, (New-Object Text.UTF8Encoding $false))
  -> 123,13,10                    <-- '{', as intended
```

And on the consuming side:

```console
>>> json.loads(path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
json.decoder.JSONDecodeError: Unexpected UTF-8 BOM (decode using utf-8-sig)
```

### Why it bites

The producer succeeds. The file opens correctly in every editor. The consumer
throws the **whole file** away. In the real case a `manifest.json` written by a
build script was discarded by the installer that read it, and the only symptom
was a single warning line — so every APK silently lost its package and version
metadata.

### The fix — both sides

```powershell
# write: no BOM, explicit
[IO.File]::WriteAllText($path, $text, (New-Object Text.UTF8Encoding $false))
```

```python
# read: accepts a file with or without a BOM
json.loads(path.read_text(encoding="utf-8-sig"))
```

Do both. Fixing only the writer leaves every file already on disk broken;
fixing only the reader leaves the next consumer — jq, a Java parser, a shell
script — to hit it instead.

Same failure mode [no-secrets](../core/no-secrets.mdc) already guards against
for `.env` files ("UTF-8 without a BOM ... with no error raised") — seen here
from the writing side, across a language boundary.

**This is a 5.1 defect.** Re-measured Oct-19-2026 on PowerShell 7.6.7: `Set-Content -Encoding UTF8`,
`Out-File -Encoding utf8` and `>` all wrote no BOM (first bytes `120,13,10`), and only
`-Encoding utf8BOM` wrote `239,187,191`. So a script that behaves in `pwsh` can still break under
5.1, and the `WriteAllText` fix below is correct in both.

`Out-File -Encoding utf8` (and therefore `>` in a 5.1 session configured for
UTF-8) does the same on 5.1: verified Oct-13-2026, first bytes `239,187,191`. A PR body
written that way and passed as `gh pr create --body-file` went up with an
invisible BOM before `## What`, so GitHub rendered the first heading as the
literal text `?## What`. Write such files with the `WriteAllText` line above.

## 3. Native arguments with embedded double quotes are mangled

Windows PowerShell 5.1 does not escape `"` when it builds the command line for a
native program (`git`, `gh`, `python`, any `.exe`). The receiving program parses
the quotes as its own: they disappear, and any space inside a quoted phrase
splits the string into separate arguments. A here-string does not help; the
damage happens when the argument is passed, not when it is written.

### Reproduction

Verified Oct-13-2026, PowerShell 5.1.26100:

```powershell
$py = 'C:\Python313\python.exe'
& $py -c "import sys; print(len(sys.argv)-1, sys.argv[1:])" 'Fix "quoted" text'
#  -> 1 ['Fix quoted text']                               quotes silently gone
& $py -c "import sys; print(len(sys.argv)-1, sys.argv[1:])" 'Fails with "No SQL Server instances found" on start'
#  -> 5 ['Fails with No', 'SQL', 'Server', 'instances', 'found on start']
```

### Why it bites

The no-space case is fully silent: a commit message or PR title quietly loses
its quotes. The split case fails far from the cause:
`git commit -m $msg` reported `error: pathspec 'SQL' did not match any file(s)`
(the words became pathspecs), and `gh pr create --body $text` reported
`unknown arguments [...]`. Nothing points at the quoting.

### The fix

Do not pass free text containing `"` as a native argument. Write it to a file
(BOM-less, see section 2) and pass the file:

```powershell
[IO.File]::WriteAllText($f, $msg, (New-Object Text.UTF8Encoding $false))
git commit -F $f
gh pr create --title "Short title" --body-file $f
gh api -X PATCH repos/<owner>/<repo>/pulls/<n> -F "body=@$f"
```

Windows PowerShell 5.1 has no switch for this, so the file route is the fix
there.

### An empty-string argument is dropped entirely

The same command-line builder also **drops** an empty string. The program
receives one argument fewer, so a flag that takes a value swallows the *next*
argument instead, or runs with its default. Verified Oct-25-2026,
PowerShell 5.1.26100.9549:

```powershell
$py = 'C:\Python313\python.exe'
$probe = "import sys; print(len(sys.argv)-1, sys.argv[1:])"
& $py -c $probe a '' b               #  -> 2 ['a', 'b']        '' vanished
& $py -c $probe a "" b               #  -> 2 ['a', 'b']
$e = ''; & $py -c $probe a $e b      #  -> 2 ['a', 'b']        a variable is no better
$x = @('-c', $probe, 'a', '', 'b'); & $py @x   #  -> 2 ['a', 'b']   nor is splatting
```

Seen as `claude -p --tools ""`: the empty value never arrived, so the tools
stayed enabled and their listings came back into the prompt, with no error.

**Fix: build the call as an argument list in Python** — `subprocess` quotes an
empty item as `""` itself:

```powershell
& $py -c "import subprocess,sys; subprocess.run([sys.executable,'-c','import sys; print(len(sys.argv)-1, sys.argv[1:])','a','','b'])"
#  -> 3 ['a', '', 'b']
```

Workarounds that also passed on 5.1.26100.9549 (same probe):

```powershell
& $py -c $probe a '""' b             #  -> 3 ['a', '', 'b']   literal quote pair
& $py --% -c "import sys; print(len(sys.argv)-1, sys.argv[1:])" a "" b
                                     #  -> 3 ['a', '', 'b']   stop-parsing: no PS variables after it
& $py -c $probe --tools= b           #  -> 2 ['--tools=', 'b'] only if the program accepts --flag=
```

Each is a 5.1-only habit: `'""'` was not re-measured on PowerShell 7 (no
`pwsh` on the test machine), whose `$PSNativeCommandArgumentPassing` rules
differ (see below), and `--%` cannot take a PowerShell variable. The Python
argument list is the one route that does not depend on the shell.

### `-F -` reads stdin, and the chain keeps going after a failed commit

`git commit -F -` takes the message from **standard input**. A here-string written after it is
a second argument, so git treats it as a pathspec and commits nothing. PowerShell does not stop
a script when a native command exits non-zero, so the `checkout`, `merge` and `branch -d` that
follow all run. Reproduced Oct-19-2026, PowerShell 7:

```powershell
git commit -q -F - @'
Subject line

Body line.
'@
#  error: pathspec 'Subject line ... Body line.' did not match any file(s)   (exit 1)
git checkout -q main; git merge --no-ff -q feature   # exit 0, "Already up to date"
git branch -d feature                                # Deleted branch feature (nothing was lost only by luck)
```

Pipe the text, or pass a file, and stop the chain on failure:

```powershell
$msg | git commit -q -F -                 # stdin, not an argument
git commit -q -F $f                       # or a BOM-less file, as above
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { throw "commit failed" }   # PowerShell 5.1 and 7
# PowerShell 7 only: git commit -q -F $f && git switch main && git merge --no-ff feature
```

The matching git-side check is in [source-control](../core/source-control.mdc): count the commits
ahead of `main` before merging.

### PowerShell 7 is the opposite trap: escapes arrive literally

PowerShell 7.3+ (`$PSNativeCommandArgumentPassing = Windows`) passes embedded
quotes through correctly, so the 5.1 habit of backslash-escaping them now
breaks the argument instead. Verified Oct-18-2026, PowerShell 7.6.7:

```powershell
& python -c "import sys; print(sys.argv[1:])" '.[] | \"\(.login) x\"'
#  -> ['.[] | \\"\\(.login) x\\"']      backslashes delivered to the program
& python -c "import sys; print(sys.argv[1:])" '.[] | "\(.login) x"'
#  -> ['.[] | "\\(.login) x"']          what jq actually needs
```

Seen as `gh api ... --jq '.[] | \"\(.login)\"'` failing with
`failed to parse jq expression ... unexpected token "\\"`. In PowerShell 7 write
the quotes plainly inside a single-quoted string; in 5.1 use a file or a filter
without embedded quotes (`--jq '.[].login'`). Check `$PSVersionTable.PSVersion`
before reusing a quoting recipe from the other edition.

## 4. A user or machine env var set now is invisible to everything already running

`[Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable('X', 'v', 'User')` and `setx X v` write the registry
(`HKCU\Environment`, or HKLM for machine) immediately and say nothing more. Every process that
was already running keeps the environment block it started with, and so does **every child it
launches**: a child copies its parent's block, it does not re-read the registry. A terminal, an
IDE, or an assistant session opened before the change reports the variable as unset, and the
usual next step (ask for the secret again, or "set it" a second time) fixes nothing.

### Reproduction

```powershell
[Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable('HUB_DEMO', 'set', 'User')
powershell -NoProfile -Command '[bool]$env:HUB_DEMO'                   # -> False  (inherited block)
(Get-ItemProperty HKCU:\Environment).HUB_DEMO                         # -> set    (the truth)
[Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable('HUB_DEMO', $null, 'User')      # clean up
```

Measured Oct-18-2026: `TG_API_ID` / `TG_API_HASH` saved to the user scope from a session, then a
fresh `powershell -NoProfile` started from that session printed `False` for both while
`HKCU:\Environment` held them (the hash at its full 32 characters).

### The fix

Before concluding a variable is missing, read the registry, not `$env:`. Refresh one in place
with `$env:X = [Environment]::GetEnvironmentVariable('X', 'User')`, and have tools that run
from long-lived shells fall back to the registry themselves (Python: `winreg` on
`HKCU\Environment`). `D:\GitHub\tools\envget` does both lookups and shows every place a name is
stored (process, `HKCU\Environment`, `HKCU\Volatile Environment`, HKLM) with the copy that wins;
`envget --diff` lists every variable whose copies disagree. The tgpost token case in
[android-deploy](../../skills/android-deploy/SKILL.md) (`401 Unauthorized`
from a terminal opened before the token was rotated) is the same defect.

## 5. Bash syntax in a PowerShell call fails at parse time, so nothing before it runs

This one is loud, not silent, but it misleads the same way: the error points at the bash line, and a
reader assumes the lines above it ran. PowerShell parses the **whole** script before executing any
of it. A bash heredoc (`cat <<'EOF'`) is a parse error, so the earlier commands never ran.
Reproduced Oct-19-2026, PowerShell 7.6.7, a script of `Write-Output 'ran first'` then `cat <<'EOF'`:

```
ParserError: hd.ps1:2
   2 |  cat <<'EOF'
     |       ~
     | Missing file specification after redirection operator.
```

`ran first` was never printed. The cure is to write the shell the tool is: use a PowerShell
here-string (`@'`...`'@`, closing marker at column 0) in a PowerShell call, or call the Bash tool
for a heredoc. When a call fails like this, assume **nothing** in it executed rather than
"everything up to the bad line".

## 6. Variable names are case-insensitive, so a loop variable can overwrite another variable

`$S` and `$s` are the same variable. A `foreach ($s in $list)` written for "screen" silently
replaces an outer `$S` that held something else, and nothing warns.

### Reproduction (Oct-30-2026, PowerShell 7.6.9 and Windows PowerShell 5.1, same result)

```powershell
$S = "00369766H004012"            # adb device serial
foreach ($s in @("03-add-sms")) { }
"after loop, `$S = $S"             # -> after loop, $S = 03-add-sms
```

### Why it bites

A screenshot script used `$S` for the device serial and `foreach ($s in $screens)` for the pages.
The first two shots (taken before the loop) worked; every later `adb -s $S ...` ran as
`adb -s 03-add-sms ...` and adb printed `unknown command 03-add-sms`, which reads like an adb
problem, not a variable problem.

### The fix

Never tell variables apart by case alone. Give loop variables distinct names (`$item`, `$page`)
and long-lived ones descriptive names (`$serial`, not `$S`).

## 7. Piping a string to a native program can prepend a BOM to its stdin

`'value' | some.exe` sends PowerShell's text through the console input encoding. When
that encoding is UTF-8 *with* a preamble, the program's stdin starts with `EF BB BF`.
Nothing errors: the receiver just sees three extra bytes before your value.

### Reproduction (Nov-10-2026, Windows PowerShell 5.1, with and without `-NoProfile`)

```powershell
[Console]::InputEncoding.GetPreamble().Length            # 3 on the affected host
'abc' | python -c "import sys; print(list(sys.stdin.buffer.read()))"
# [239, 187, 191, 97, 98, 99, ...]
```

`$OutputEncoding` is not the cause: it read `US-ASCII` (and setting it to BOM-less UTF-8
changed nothing) while the BOM still arrived.

### Why it bites

A secret piped this way never equals itself. Seen Nov-09-2026: a script compared a piped
password with the known-good one, got `False`, and refused to deploy it; the same value
passed to `smtplib.login` raised `UnicodeEncodeError` (the BOM decodes as non-ASCII).
Both looked like a wrong password.

### The fix

- Pass values to native programs in an environment variable or a file, not on a pipe:
  `$env:X = 'abc'` then read `os.environ['X']` delivers the exact bytes `[97, 98, 99]`.
- If you must pipe, set `[Console]::InputEncoding = New-Object System.Text.UTF8Encoding $false`
  first. That removes the BOM but the pipe still appends `\r\n` (`[97, 98, 99, 13, 10]`),
  so strip it on the reading side.
- On the reading side, accept both:
  `sys.stdin.buffer.read().removeprefix(b"\xef\xbb\xbf").decode().strip()`.

## Verify

Run these on your own machine; none needs a project:

```powershell
# 1. array -match leaves $Matches unset
$Matches = $null
@("versionName='1.2'", "other") -match "versionName='([^']+)'" | Out-Null
if ($null -eq $Matches) { "BUG: array -match did not set `$Matches" }

# 2. -Encoding UTF8 emits EF BB BF
"{}" | Set-Content "$env:TEMP\bom.json" -Encoding UTF8
[IO.File]::ReadAllBytes("$env:TEMP\bom.json")[0..2] -join ','   # -> 239,187,191

# 3. embedded quotes split a native argument
& python -c "import sys; print(len(sys.argv)-1, sys.argv[1:])" 'a "b c" d'   # -> 2 ['a b', 'c d']
& python -c "import sys; print(len(sys.argv)-1, sys.argv[1:])" a '' b          # -> 2 ['a', 'b']

# 4. a user env var set now does not reach a child of this shell
[Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable('HUB_DEMO', 'set', 'User')
powershell -NoProfile -Command '[bool]$env:HUB_DEMO'                   # -> False
[Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable('HUB_DEMO', $null, 'User')

# 6. a differently-cased loop variable overwrites the outer one
$S = 'serial'; foreach ($s in @('page')) { }; $S                    # -> page
```

Expect `BUG: array -match did not set $Matches`, `239,187,191`, `2 ['a b', 'c d']`,
`2 ['a', 'b']` and `page`. If you
see them, the defects are live in your shell.
